 { In the first chapter, we delved into the concept of weak squares as a foundation of positional play. Building upon this, we now turn our attention to a closely related concept: the 'Outpost'. An outpost is a square on the fourth to seventh rank, which is protected by a pawn and not attackable by any of the opponent's pawns. In this example, the e5 square is an outpost because of White's supporting pawn on d4. On the other hand, Black's knight on e4 does not constitute an outpost, as it remains vulnerable to White's pawn advance f2-f3. This chapter will cover several key aspects of outposts: (1) General guidelines, including how to select the right outpost (2) Techniques for maneuvering pieces to outposts (3) Strategies for creating outposts (4) Fighting for, or defending against, outposts (5) The concept of 'Power Outposts', a particularly strong type of outpost (6) 'Pseudo outposts', which are not technically outposts, but function like one You will develop your skill in situations where the play revolves around an outpost, or in some cases several outposts. After the theory section, you will have the opportunity to test your learning by completing a themed puzzle set. Let's begin! } 1. { -KEY- }  
